# The town's budget, costs and rules are made up for the lesson.
lesson history-how-a-town-is-run v=1 format=teach grade=5 unit=6 subject=history levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "How a town is run"
  goal "Say how a town council decides by voting, read what a town spends its money on, and give the reason for a town's rule."

  look {
    scene 23x12 {
      council c hands=[1, 1, 0, 1, 1, 0, 1] box=0 motion="A crossing by the school" count=1 at=canvas(1, 0)
    }
    say """
      A town is run by a council, a group of people chosen by the town's voters. The council meets,
      talks, and votes: an idea passes when more than half vote for it. It makes rules, such as where
      cars may park, and it spends the town's money on roads, parks, bins and the library. Every rule
      is there to solve a problem, and a fair council listens to everyone the rule affects.
      """
  }
  do {
    level easy {
      worked history.council-passes k=0
    }
    show history.council-passes k=2 easy-k=1 hard-k=4
    show history.town-budget k=2 easy-k=0 hard-k=4
    show history.rule-reasons k=2 easy-k=0 hard-k=4
    show history.assembly-vote k=4 easy-k=0 hard-k=5
    show history.council-passes k=3 easy-k=2 hard-k=5
    show history.town-budget k=3 easy-k=1 hard-k=5
    show history.rule-reasons k=3 easy-k=1 hard-k=5
    show history.town-budget k=1 easy-k=2 hard-k=3
    level hard {
      show history.rule-reasons k=3 level=medium
    }
  }
  try stars=2 {
    show history.majority-fair k=0 hard-k=1
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show history.budget-puzzle k=0 hard-k=1
  }
  remember {
    say "A council decides by votes, spends the town's money, and makes rules, each to solve a problem."
  }

  grown-ups """
    The idea is how the place a child lives is run: people are chosen to decide, they vote, they
    make rules and they spend money raised from the town. The way in counts a council's vote; the
    core reads a town's spending, says what a rule is for, and counts votes again. The two-star
    question asks what a fair council owes the people who lost a vote; there is no single right
    answer in real life, and the lesson asks only for listening. The wrong idea to watch for is
    "rules are just to stop fun". If the child is stuck, pick a rule at home and ask what would go
    wrong without it. Off the page, find your own council's website: who sits on it, and what did
    it last decide? Japan's 社会 for grade six teaches 「国民生活には地方公共団体や国の政治の働きが反映していること」,
    how local and national government shape people's lives (the course of study as read in its 2008
    edition). China's 道德与法治 in the upper grades takes up how the people take part in running
    public life (recalled from the textbook series). The towns, votes and costs are made up.
    Russia teaches how the country is governed in обществознание, in later grades in the edition our
    audit read.
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level a worked example counts a clear vote, the budget questions read one row or add two, the rules are about safety, and the council's votes pass or fail by a lot."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the votes pass or fail by one, the budget questions compare sums of rows, the rules are about permission and prices, and the three-star town spends its money on two or three projects."
  }
}
